SA=" (Select all that apply.)"
C2 = [
("Assessment","A parent asks what an echocardiogram will be like for their 3-year-old. Which response is best?",["\"It uses sound waves, doesn't hurt, and your child will need to lie still.\""],["\"Your child will need an IV dye and must fast overnight.\"","\"It uses a lot of radiation, so you'll need to leave the room.\"","\"A small tube will be threaded through the groin.\""],
 "Echo is noninvasive ultrasound with no radiation. Lying still is the main challenge, and young children may need sedation. A catheter through the groin is a cardiac cath.","\"Echo = Easy: sound waves, no needles, no rays.\""),
("Assessment","Which nursing actions are appropriate AFTER a cardiac catheterization?"+SA,["Keep the affected leg straight as ordered","Check the insertion site for bleeding","Check pulses below the insertion site","Compare temperature and color of both legs"],["Encourage walking right away","Bend the leg to prevent stiffness"],
 "Bleeding and loss of circulation to the leg are the key risks. The leg stays straight for several hours as ordered.","\"Site, Pulse, Color, Warmth, and keep it Straight.\""),
("Assessment","The nurse finds bleeding at a child's femoral cath site. What is the FIRST action?",["Apply direct, continuous pressure just above the insertion site"],["Elevate the head of the bed","Remove the dressing to look","Call the parents"],
 "Direct pressure (about 1 inch above the percutaneous site) stops arterial bleeding. Then notify the provider.","\"Bleeding? Press first, page second.\""),
("Assessment","Which history findings suggest heart disease in a child?"+SA,["Poor weight gain","Tiring or sweating with feeds","Frequent respiratory infections","Exercise intolerance","Family history of heart defects or sudden death"],["Early tooth eruption","Curly hair"],
 "Growth, feeding, infections, activity tolerance, and family history are core parts of the cardiac history.","\"Grow, Eat, Breathe, Play, Family.\""),
("Assessment","What is the purpose of exercise testing in a child with a heart condition?",["To see how the heart rhythm, blood pressure, and endurance respond to exercise"],["To diagnose a septal defect","To measure lung volumes","To check for strep"],
 "Exercise testing (treadmill or bike) checks the heart's response to effort and exercise capacity.","\"Treadmill test = how the heart handles hustle.\""),
("VSD","A 6-week-old with a large VSD tires during feeds and is not gaining weight. Which plan is best?",["High-calorie formula (24–30 kcal/oz) in small, frequent feeds, with NG feeding if the infant tires"],["Dilute the formula to reduce fluid","Feed large volumes every 6 hours","Stop feeds and give IV fluids only"],
 "Infants in heart failure burn more calories and tire easily. Pack in calories without adding volume.","\"Pack the calories, not the volume: 24-27-30.\""),
("VSD","Which findings would the nurse expect in a 6-week-old with a LARGE VSD?"+SA,["Tachypnea","Sweating during feeds","Poor weight gain","Hepatomegaly"],["Cyanosis at birth","Bradycardia"],
 "As lung resistance falls, extra blood floods the lungs → heart failure at about 4–8 weeks. VSD is acyanotic.","\"Big VSD at 6 weeks: Fast breathing, Sweaty, Skinny, Swollen liver.\""),
("VSD","A child with a large VSD was never repaired and now has cyanosis. What has most likely happened?",["Pulmonary hypertension has reversed the shunt to right-to-left (Eisenmenger)"],["The VSD closed on its own","The child developed asthma","The ductus arteriosus reopened"],
 "Years of extra lung flow damage the lung vessels → pulmonary HTN → right-sided pressure exceeds left → blood shunts right-to-left → cyanosis.","\"Flooded lungs Fight back → Flip the shunt.\""),
("VSD","After surgical VSD repair with a patch, how long is endocarditis prophylaxis needed (assuming no residual leak)?",["6 months after repair"],["Lifelong","Never","Only until discharge"],
 "Prosthetic material needs about 6 months to be covered by heart tissue. A residual leak next to the patch would mean ongoing prophylaxis.","\"Patch needs 6 months to heal over.\""),
("ASD","A child had an ASD closed with a device in the cath lab. Which medication teaching is expected?",["Low-dose aspirin for about 6 months"],["Warfarin for life","Digoxin for life","No medicines are ever used"],
 "Low-dose aspirin prevents clots on the device while heart tissue grows over it.","\"New device? Aspirin for 6 months.\""),
("ASD","The parent of a 4-year-old with a small ASD asks about soccer. Which response is correct?",["\"No exercise restriction is needed.\""],["\"No sports until age 18.\"","\"Only non-contact sports, and only with oxygen.\"","\"Sports are allowed only after surgery.\""],
 "Chapter 38: exercise restriction is unnecessary with an ASD.","\"ASD = A Sporty Defect: let them play.\""),
("ASD","Which are complications of an untreated large ASD later in life?"+SA,["Right ventricular enlargement and failure","Pulmonary hypertension","Atrial arrhythmias","A clot crossing to the body (paradoxical embolus)"],["Coronary artery aneurysm","Left ventricular outflow obstruction"],
 "Extra right-sided volume wears out the right heart; clots can cross the hole into the systemic circulation.","\"Right side wears out, clots sneak across.\""),
("PDA","A premature infant is receiving indomethacin for a PDA. Which assessment is the priority?",["Urine output (renal effects)"],["Pupil size","Bowel sounds only","Hearing"],
 "Indomethacin can decrease kidney blood flow (low urine output) and affect bleeding and the GI tract. Monitor urine output closely.","\"Indomethacin = Watch the Pee.\""),
("PDA","Why does a PDA increase blood flow to the lungs after birth?",["Aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure, so blood flows aorta → pulmonary artery"],["The foramen ovale stays open","Pulmonary pressure becomes higher than aortic pressure","Blood flows from the right atrium to the left atrium"],
 "As lung resistance falls after birth, blood flows from the high-pressure aorta into the pulmonary artery and recirculates through the lungs.","\"High to Low: Aorta pushes into the lungs.\""),
("PDA","Which findings suggest a significant PDA in a premature infant?"+SA,["Bounding pulses","Wide pulse pressure","Increasing oxygen needs","Tachypnea"],["Weak, thready pulses","Narrow pulse pressure"],
 "Runoff from the aorta into the pulmonary artery causes bounding pulses and a wide pulse pressure; lung overflow causes respiratory distress.","\"PDA = Bounce, Wide, Breathless.\""),
("PDA","A healthy 4-month-old born at TERM still has a small PDA. Which plan is expected?",["Follow for closure in the cath lab (device) or surgery; indomethacin won't work"],["Indomethacin now","Prostaglandin E1 infusion","No follow-up needed"],
 "Indomethacin/ibuprofen only works in preemies. Term infants have device (coil/plug) closure or surgical ligation.","\"Indomethacin is for Infants born early only.\""),
("Pulmonic stenosis","A newborn with CRITICAL pulmonic stenosis is cyanotic. Why?",["Blood shunts right-to-left across the foramen ovale"],["Blood shunts left-to-right through a VSD","The aorta is narrowed","The newborn has pneumonia"],
 "High right-sided pressure pushes blood through the foramen ovale to the left side, skipping the lungs → cyanosis.","\"Right side Blocked → blood takes the Back door (foramen ovale).\""),
("Pulmonic stenosis","A child with pulmonic stenosis also has short stature and wide-set eyes. Which syndrome should the nurse suspect?",["Noonan syndrome"],["Marfan syndrome","Kawasaki disease","Down syndrome"],
 "Noonan syndrome: valvular pulmonic stenosis and HCM, with short stature.","\"Noonan = PS + short Nursery-sized stature.\""),
("Pulmonic stenosis","Severe pulmonic stenosis increases right ventricular pressure. Which findings can result?"+SA,["Right ventricular hypertrophy","Signs of right-sided heart failure (hepatomegaly, edema)","Cyanosis if severe"],["Left ventricular hypertrophy","Pulmonary edema as the first sign"],
 "The right side works harder → RVH → right-sided failure (fluid backs up into the body). Pulmonary edema is left-sided.","\"PS = Pressure on the Right → Right-sided failure.\""),
("Aortic stenosis","Which blood pressure finding is typical of aortic stenosis?",["Narrow pulse pressure"],["Wide pulse pressure","Higher BP in the legs than the arms","Very high diastolic BP only"],
 "Limited outflow through the narrow valve makes a small difference between systolic and diastolic BP. Wide pulse pressure is PDA.","\"AS = A Squeezed pulse pressure.\""),
("Aortic stenosis","A newborn with critical aortic stenosis is most at risk for:",["Low cardiac output and shock"],["Polycythemia","Hypertension in the legs","Endocarditis in the first hour"],
 "Critical AS blocks blood leaving the left ventricle → heart failure, low output, shock. It needs urgent balloon valvuloplasty.","\"Critical AS = the Exit is blocked → shock.\""),
("Aortic stenosis","A teen with aortic stenosis is started on a beta blocker. Which teaching is correct?",["\"Don't stop it suddenly, and report dizziness or a slow pulse.\""],["\"Take it only when you have chest pain.\"","\"Double the dose before sports.\"","\"Stop it if you feel tired.\""],
 "Beta blockers slow the heart and reduce workload. Stopping abruptly can cause rebound tachycardia.","\"Beta = Be steady: no sudden stops.\""),
("Aortic stenosis","A child with aortic stenosis should seek immediate evaluation for which symptoms?"+SA,["Chest pain with exercise","Fainting","Dizziness","Palpitations"],["Runny nose","Mild sunburn"],
 "Exertional chest pain and syncope can come before sudden death in AS.","\"AS = Avoid Sudden collapse: Chest pain, Faint, Dizzy, Flutters.\""),
("Coarctation","An 8-year-old's BP is 128/80 in the right arm and 90/60 in the leg. How should the nurse interpret this?",["This suggests coarctation; normally leg BP is equal to or higher than arm BP"],["This is normal for a child","The cuff in the arm is too large","This suggests aortic stenosis"],
 "In children the leg BP is normally the same or higher. A lower leg BP points to an obstruction (coarctation).","\"Legs should never lose to arms.\""),
("Coarctation","After coarctation repair, which finding needs the closest monitoring?",["Blood pressure (hypertension and recoarctation)"],["Hearing","Blood glucose","Visual acuity"],
 "Older children may stay hypertensive after repair, and restenosis can occur. BP checks are lifelong.","\"Fix the coarct, then Follow BP forever.\""),
("Coarctation","A 5-day-old with critical coarctation is likely to show which findings?"+SA,["Poor feeding","Tachypnea","Weak femoral pulses","Cool or mottled legs"],["Bounding femoral pulses","Hypertension in the legs"],
 "When the ductus closes, blood flow to the lower body drops → poor perfusion and heart failure. PGE1 reopens the ductus.","\"Duct closes → legs go cold → baby crashes.\""),
("Coarctation","A child with coarctation has frequent nosebleeds. Why?",["High blood pressure in the upper body"],["Low platelets","Dry air only","A bleeding disorder caused by the aorta"],
 "Pressure is high above the narrowing, causing headaches and nosebleeds.","\"High pressure upstairs → head trouble: headache, nosebleed, stroke.\""),
("Coarctation","Which syndrome is most associated with coarctation of the aorta?",["Turner syndrome"],["Down syndrome","DiGeorge syndrome","Williams syndrome"],
 "Turner (monosomy X): bicuspid aortic valve and coarctation. Down syndrome = AV canal.","\"Turner Turns the aorta.\""),
("Tricuspid atresia","In tricuspid atresia, which chamber is underdeveloped?",["Right ventricle"],["Left ventricle","Left atrium","None; all chambers are normal"],
 "No blood enters the right ventricle from the right atrium, so it stays small. The left ventricle does all the work.","\"No Tricuspid = Tiny right ventricle.\""),
("Tricuspid atresia","An infant with tricuspid atresia is on a prostaglandin E1 infusion. Which nursing actions are appropriate?"+SA,["Monitor for apnea","Keep continuous pulse oximetry","Keep the IV line patent","Watch for fever and flushing"],["Stop the infusion once the saturation improves","Give it together with indomethacin"],
 "PGE1 keeps the ductus open until surgery. Side effects include apnea, fever, flushing, and low BP. Stopping it can close the ductus.","\"PGE1: Patent ductus, Pause in breathing, Pink flush, Pyrexia.\""),
("Tricuspid atresia","What is the long-term surgical plan for tricuspid atresia?",["Staged palliation: shunt, then Glenn (4–6 months), then Fontan (2–4 years)"],["One-time VSD patch","Indomethacin only","Balloon valvuloplasty only"],
 "Single-ventricle hearts are rerouted in stages so venous blood flows directly to the lungs.","\"Superior first, Inferior last: Glenn (SVC) then Fontan (IVC).\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","Which finding indicates LONG-STANDING hypoxemia in a child with TOF?",["Clubbing of the fingers"],["Bounding pulses","Hypertension in the arms","Pale, pink nail beds"],
 "Clubbing develops after months of low oxygen. It's on the addendum's TOF list.","\"Clubs take time: months of blue.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","A child with uncorrected TOF has a hemoglobin of 20 g/dL and a hematocrit of 62%. What is the priority teaching?",["Keep the child well hydrated, especially during illness or hot weather"],["Restrict fluids to protect the heart","Start iron supplements","No teaching is needed; these values are normal"],
 "This is compensatory polycythemia. Thick blood plus dehydration raises the risk of clots and stroke.","\"Thick blood + dry = clot risk: keep them drinking.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","Which chest x-ray finding is classic for TOF?",["Boot-shaped heart with decreased pulmonary vascular markings"],["Egg-shaped heart","Rib notching","Enlarged heart with increased pulmonary markings"],
 "Pulmonary stenosis means less blood reaches the lungs (fewer markings), and RVH tips the apex up into a boot shape.","\"Boot = TOF, Egg = TGA, Notches = Coarct.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","What should the nurse teach the parents of an infant with TOF about tet spells?"+SA,["Place the infant in a knee-chest position","Keep the infant calm","Call emergency services if the spell doesn't resolve quickly","Avoid long periods of crying"],["Lay the infant flat on the back","Give a large bottle right away"],
 "Knee-chest raises systemic resistance, pushing more blood to the lungs. Prolonged spells can lead to syncope or worse.","\"Tet spell? Tuck like a frog, then call for help.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","Why does the amount of cyanosis vary among infants with TOF?",["It depends on how severe the pulmonary stenosis is"],["It depends on the size of the ASD","It depends on the infant's blood type","It depends on the room temperature only"],
 "More obstruction → more blood crosses the VSD right-to-left → more cyanosis. Mild obstruction = \"pink tet.\"","\"More Pulmonary stenosis = More Purple.\""),
("Heart failure","An infant on digoxin is vomiting and has a heart rate of 80. What should the nurse suspect?",["Digoxin toxicity: hold the dose and notify the provider"],["Normal side effects; give the next dose","A viral infection only","Dehydration from overfeeding"],
 "Early digoxin toxicity in infants: vomiting, poor feeding, and bradycardia. Low potassium increases the risk.","\"Dig toxic = Dizzy, Dumping (vomiting), Dropping heart rate.\""),
("Heart failure","What is the BEST way for parents to monitor fluid status at home in an infant on furosemide?",["Daily weight on the same scale, at the same time"],["Count breaths once a week","Check the color of the stool","Measure the head circumference"],
 "A rapid weight gain means fluid is building up. Also count wet diapers.","\"Same Scale, Same time, Same clothes.\""),
("Heart failure","Which feeding strategies help an infant with heart failure?"+SA,["Small, frequent feedings","A soft nipple with a larger opening","Feeding in a semi-upright position","Limiting each feed to about 20–30 minutes, then gavage the rest if ordered"],["Large feedings every 6 hours","Diluting the formula"],
 "These save the infant's energy while maximizing calories.","\"Small, Soft, Sitting up, Stop at 30.\""),
("Heart failure","Before giving captopril (an ACE inhibitor) to an infant with heart failure, which assessment is the priority?",["Blood pressure"],["Temperature","Blood glucose","Head circumference"],
 "ACE inhibitors dilate vessels to reduce afterload and can cause hypotension. They can also raise potassium.","\"ACE = Afterload Cut, check the pressure.\""),
("Heart failure","Which position is best for an infant with heart failure and tachypnea?",["Semi-upright (semi-Fowler)"],["Flat on the back","Trendelenburg (head down)","Prone with the head lower than the feet"],
 "Upright positioning lets the lungs expand and decreases the work of breathing.","\"Heads up for a tired heart.\""),
("Heart failure","Which actions reduce the heart's oxygen demand in an infant with heart failure?"+SA,["Cluster care to allow rest","Keep a neutral thermal environment","Treat fever promptly","Minimize prolonged crying"],["Keep the room cold","Wake the infant often to stimulate"],
 "Cold, fever, crying, and frequent handling all increase the heart's workload.","\"Rest, Warm, Cool fever, Calm.\""),
("Rheumatic fever","A child with rheumatic fever has painful, swollen joints. Which treatment helps the joint inflammation?",["An anti-inflammatory such as aspirin or another NSAID, as prescribed"],["IVIG","Indomethacin to close the ductus","Ice baths every hour"],
 "Anti-inflammatory therapy treats the arthritis; antibiotics eradicate the strep. Joints heal without lasting damage.","\"Joints heal, the Heart remembers.\""),
("Rheumatic fever","What is the purpose of regular benzathine penicillin injections after rheumatic fever?",["To prevent repeat strep infections that could cause more heart valve damage"],["To treat the chorea","To close a VSD","To lower the platelet count"],
 "Secondary prophylaxis prevents recurrences. Each attack can worsen valve damage.","\"Prevent the repeat, protect the valves.\""),
("Rheumatic fever","Which nursing actions are appropriate for a child with Sydenham chorea?"+SA,["Protect from injury, for example with padded side rails","Help with eating and writing","Reassure the family that the movements are temporary","Reduce stress"],["Restrain the child's arms","Tell the child to try harder to hold still"],
 "The movements are involuntary and get worse with stress. They gradually resolve.","\"Chorea care: Safe, Help, Reassure, Calm.\""),
("Kawasaki","Which description of the FEVER in Kawasaki disease is correct?",["High fever for 5 or more days that doesn't respond to antipyretics or antibiotics"],["Low-grade fever for 1 day","Fever only at night","Fever that breaks with one dose of acetaminophen"],
 "The addendum: high fever that doesn't respond to therapy. Children are also extremely irritable.","\"BURN for 5: the fever that won't quit.\""),
("Kawasaki","A child with Kawasaki disease is taking aspirin. Which teaching is essential?",["Report exposure to flu or chickenpox, and get the flu vaccine (Reye syndrome risk)"],["Give extra aspirin for any fever","Aspirin can be stopped whenever the child feels better","Start ibuprofen along with the aspirin"],
 "Aspirin plus influenza or varicella can cause Reye syndrome. Ibuprofen can block aspirin's antiplatelet effect. (Note: your addendum slide lists ibuprofen; your study guide and AHA say aspirin.)","\"Aspirin kid: no Flu, no Pox.\""),
("Kawasaki","A child is in the late (convalescent) phase of Kawasaki disease. Which teaching is correct?"+SA,["Peeling of the fingers and toes is expected","Platelets rise in this phase, increasing the clot risk","Keep all follow-up echocardiograms"],["Peeling means a new infection","Follow-up can stop once the fever is gone"],
 "Late phase = peeling, thrombocytosis, and possible aneurysms. Echo follow-up continues.","\"Late = Peel, Platelets, Pouches (aneurysms).\""),
]
